---
import BrandHead from "../components/BrandHead.astro";
import BrandLogo from "../components/BrandLogo.astro";
import "../styles/global.css";

const url = Astro.url;
const title = "How steekem works — peer-to-peer browser sharing";
const description = "How steekem sends text, Wi-Fi, passwords, and credit cards directly browser-to-browser over WebRTC, encrypted end-to-end with a key that lives in the link, and no server holding your bytes.";
const canonical = `${url.origin}/how-it-works`;
const ogImage = `${url.origin}/og.png`;
const buildCommit = __BUILD_COMMIT__;
const buildShort = buildCommit === "unknown" ? "unknown" : buildCommit.slice(0, 7);
const buildCommitUrl = buildCommit === "unknown" ? null : `https://github.com/JanJetze/steekem/commit/${buildCommit}`;
const buildRunUrl = __BUILD_RUN_URL__;
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>{title}</title>
    <meta name="description" content={description} />
    <link rel="canonical" href={canonical} />
    <meta property="og:title" content={title} />
    <meta property="og:description" content={description} />
    <meta property="og:type" content="article" />
    <meta property="og:url" content={canonical} />
    <meta property="og:image" content={ogImage} />
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image" />
    <meta name="twitter:title" content={title} />
    <meta name="twitter:description" content={description} />
    <meta name="twitter:image" content={ogImage} />
    <BrandHead />
  </head>
  <body>
    <div class="shell wide">
      <BrandLogo href="/" />

      <article class="prose">
        <h1>How steekem works</h1>

        <p>
          You open one of the tools — <a href="/text">text</a>,
          <a href="/wifi">Wi-Fi</a>, <a href="/password">password</a>, or
          <a href="/card">credit card</a> — and pick what to share. Steekem
          hands you a 6-character code and a link. The other person opens that
          link, and your two browsers find each other through a tiny signaling
          worker — the only server steekem runs. From there the browsers
          negotiate a direct WebRTC connection and the bytes flow straight
          between them, encrypted by the same DTLS layer your browser uses for
          video calls. There is no upload. Nothing you share ever lands on a
          server we control.
        </p>

        <h2>Why peer-to-peer</h2>
        <p>
          Most "send a file" or "share a password" tools work by asking you to
          upload to a server, then issuing a link other people can use to
          download. The server holds your data — for minutes, hours, sometimes
          longer. That server can be subpoenaed, breached, or operated by
          someone whose privacy promises don't survive a change of CEO. Even
          when the file is encrypted at rest, the operator usually holds the
          key.
        </p>
        <p>
          Peer-to-peer skips that step. The two browsers exchange a handful of
          small messages, just enough to find each other through routers and
          firewalls, then connect directly. Bytes flow from one device to the
          other. We don't have a copy. The room code and a few packets of
          routing metadata are everything we ever see, and only for the seconds
          the connection is being set up.
        </p>

        <h2>What happens when you share something</h2>
        <p>The full flow looks like this:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong>You pick something to share.</strong> Each tool has its own
            page tuned to the type of thing — a text snippet, Wi-Fi
            credentials, a login, a credit card.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Steekem generates a room code.</strong> Six characters from
            a non-confusable alphabet
            (<code>abcdefghjkmnpqrstuvwxyz23456789</code> — no
            <code>i</code>/<code>l</code>/<code>o</code>/<code>0</code>/<code>1</code>).
            About 887 million combinations. The code is generated entirely in
            your browser using <code>crypto.getRandomValues</code>; we never see
            it server-side until your browser opens a WebSocket to that room.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>You share the link.</strong> The link is
            <code>/r#abc123</code> — the room code lives in the URL fragment,
            which browsers do <em>not</em> send to servers. Even our signaling
            worker, which receives the WebSocket connection on
            <code>/room/abc123</code>, only sees the path, not anything you'd
            consider sensitive.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The other person opens the link and taps Receive.</strong>
            Opening the page alone does nothing — link previews and URL
            scanners load it too. Only the tap connects their browser to the
            same room on the signaling worker. The worker's job
            at this point is exactly: "tell each of you that the other has
            arrived."
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The browsers negotiate.</strong> This is the
            <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/WebRTC_API" rel="noopener">WebRTC</a>
            part. They exchange Session Description Protocol (SDP) offers and
            ICE candidates — essentially "here's a list of network addresses I
            can be reached at; pick one" — and run a DTLS handshake to set up
            encryption keys.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>They open a DataChannel.</strong> WebRTC's equivalent of a
            TCP connection, but encrypted end-to-end and tunneled through
            whatever NAT/firewall traversal trick worked. Once it's open, the
            two browsers can send arbitrary bytes to each other directly.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The transfer happens.</strong> What this looks like depends
            on the tool — see below.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>It ends.</strong> The channel closes. The signaling worker
            drops the room. Nothing persists.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <h2>The signaling layer</h2>
        <p>
          The only server steekem runs is a small
          <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/" rel="noopener">Cloudflare Worker</a>
          paired with a
          <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/" rel="noopener">Durable Object</a>
          per active room. A Durable Object is a tiny isolated process
          Cloudflare runs at a single point — one per room ID. Two browsers
          using the same room land in the same instance.
        </p>
        <p>What it does:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Accepts WebSocket connections at <code>/room/&lt;code&gt;</code>.</li>
          <li>
            Pairs the first two browsers that connect with the same code,
            tagging one as the sender and the other as the receiver.
          </li>
          <li>
            Relays the small handshake messages they need to find each other on
            the network — SDP descriptions, ICE candidates. These are about
            routing, not content.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>What it doesn't do:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Touch your bytes. The data bypasses it entirely.</li>
          <li>Authenticate anything. There aren't accounts to authenticate against.</li>
          <li>
            Log connections. Room state lives in memory, and the room dies as
            soon as both clients leave.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Single-use snippets: text, Wi-Fi, password, card</h2>
        <p>
          When a receiver connects, the sender first announces only what kind
          of share is waiting — not its contents:
        </p>
        <pre class="code-block">{`{ "type": "data-offer", "kind": "wifi" }`}</pre>
        <p>
          The receiver answers with <code>data-accept</code> (after a PIN
          check, if the sender asked for one), and only then does the sender
          release the actual payload in a single message. The data is small
          and inline, so it doesn't need chunking:
        </p>
        <pre class="code-block">{`{ "type": "data-payload", "data": {
    "kind": "wifi",
    "ssid": "...",
    "password": "...",
    "security": "WPA",
    "hidden": false
} }`}</pre>
        <p>
          Because nothing connects until someone taps <em>Receive</em>, link
          previews, URL scanners in chat and email tools, or a tab opened by
          accident can't consume a single-use share before the intended person
          sees it.
        </p>
        <p>
          The receiver renders it appropriately — a scannable Wi-Fi QR code, a
          labeled credentials card, a card-shaped credit-card preview. The
          sender flips the session to "delivered" as soon as the payload is
          sent, writes a localStorage marker so anyone who later opens the
          same <code>/r#code</code> link sees "this share has already been
          used," and closes the connection. That later visitor learns only
          the kind of share — never the SSID, username or last card digits.
        </p>
        <p>The sender side has its own guards:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Optional PIN.</strong> When composing, the sender can set
            a 4-digit PIN, to pass on another way — out loud or in a call. The
            share link then ends in <code>.p</code>, so the receive page asks
            for the PIN together with the Receive tap. The receiver sends it
            as <code>pin-check</code> right after connecting; a wrong PIN
            disconnects immediately, so nobody holds the room while guessing.
            The sender verifies it and only then honors the accept. Three
            wrong attempts, from any device, cancel the share. Protects
            against forwarded links and other people reading the same chat.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Who has it open.</strong> The receiver reports a rough
            device label ("iPhone · Safari"), so the sender can see where the
            share is about to go. It's self-reported, so it's a hint, not proof.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Cancel and expiry.</strong> The sender can cancel a share at
            any time, and an unopened share cancels itself after 15 minutes.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          A connected receiver that sits on a file's Accept prompt for two
          minutes disconnects on its own. A room holds one sender and one
          receiver, so a forgotten tab would otherwise lock the intended
          receiver out.
        </p>
        <p>The receiver-side card has three security guards:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Reveal with countdown.</strong> Sensitive fields (password,
            card number, CVC) are dotted by default. Tap <em>Reveal</em> and
            they're shown for five seconds, then hidden again. Stops the
            over-the-shoulder leak when you put your phone down.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Auto-discard after five minutes.</strong> Even if you never
            interact, the snippet vanishes from the page after five minutes
            total. The card flips to "Discarded" and Reveal/Copy stop working.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Manual discard.</strong> A <em>Discard now</em> link wipes
            immediately.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          For passwords specifically, there's also a "Save to password manager"
          button that calls the
          <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Credential_Management_API" rel="noopener">Credential Management API</a>,
          triggering the browser's native save-credential prompt. Browser
          extensions (1Password, Bitwarden) intercept that prompt and let you
          save into your real vault with the right URL. Available where the API
          is supported (Chromium-based browsers); silently hidden where it's
          not.
        </p>

        <h2>End-to-end encryption</h2>
        <p>
          Every byte that flows over a steekem DataChannel is encrypted by
          <a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc6347" rel="noopener">DTLS</a>
          — the same protocol your browser uses for the audio and video in a
          video call. The handshake happens between your two browsers, and the
          keys are unique to each connection and discarded when the channel
          closes.
        </p>
        <p>
          DTLS alone has a known weak spot, and we'd rather name it than hide
          it: the handshake is relayed by our signaling worker. A hostile
          worker could swap in its own certificates and sit in the middle.
          That's why the share link carries a second secret after the code:
        </p>
        <pre class="code-block">{`https://steekem.com/r#abc123.Gk3pQ9vXz2mN8rTbW5yLhA`}</pre>
        <p>
          Everything after <code>#</code> is the URL fragment, which browsers
          never send to any server. Both browsers derive an AES-256-GCM key
          from that secret (HKDF, with the room code as salt) and encrypt every
          message on the channel with it, on top of DTLS. Whatever the worker
          does to the handshake, it only ever sees ciphertext. The secret is
          generated fresh for each share and lives in the sender's tab only.
        </p>
        <p>
          A receiver who <em>types</em> the code instead of opening the link
          doesn't have that secret, so there the channel is DTLS only. To close
          the gap, both screens show the same four words, derived from the
          certificate fingerprints on each side. The sender compares them with
          the receiver before releasing anything; a browser sitting in the
          middle would produce different words on the two screens.
        </p>
        <p>
          We have no way to decrypt anything you send. The link secret never
          reaches us, and the words make a silent middleman visible.
        </p>

        <h2>Open source</h2>
        <p>
          Everything above is checkable. The web app, the signaling worker and
          the WebRTC engine with its encryption live in one repository under
          the AGPL-3.0 license:
          <a href="https://github.com/JanJetze/steekem" rel="noopener">github.com/JanJetze/steekem</a>.
          Bug reports and pull requests are welcome.
        </p>

        <h2>Which build you are running</h2>
        <p>
          Open code only proves what the code does, not that this site serves
          it. You still have to trust the server that hands you the
          JavaScript; that is true of every browser-based tool, and we would
          rather say it than pretend otherwise. What we can do is make the
          chain visible:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Deploys happen only from GitHub Actions, never from a laptop. The
            workflow runs are public, so anyone can see which commit was built
            and where it went.
          </li>
          <li>
            Every build is stamped with its commit. This page was built from
            {buildCommitUrl
              ? <a href={buildCommitUrl} rel="noopener"><code>{buildShort}</code></a>
              : <code>{buildShort}</code>}{buildRunUrl && <> by <a href={buildRunUrl} rel="noopener">this run</a></>}.
            The same information is at <a href="/version.json"><code>/version.json</code></a>.
          </li>
          <li>
            The bundle you downloaded is what runs. Your browser's developer
            tools show every request this page makes; there should be none to
            any host but this one and, once you share something, the signaling
            worker.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>What we don't see, log, or store</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            What you send. There's no point in our code that reads it.
          </li>
          <li>
            Who you are. No accounts, no IPs stored, no fingerprinting, no
            analytics on what you share.
          </li>
          <li>
            Session metadata. The Durable Object holds room state in memory
            only; it dies when both clients leave.
          </li>
          <li>
            A copy of your data on a server. There's no "deleted after 24
            hours" — nothing was uploaded in the first place.
          </li>
          <li>
            The room code, once you're done with it. It's only kept for as
            long as the WebSocket connections are open.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          One thing does live somewhere: in your own browser. The sender keeps
          a localStorage marker (<code>steekem-room-&lt;code&gt;</code>) so a
          refreshed tab can still show what you sent and confirm it went
          through. It never leaves your machine, and it ages out after seven
          days.
        </p>

        <h2>What's still rough</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Both tabs need to stay open during a transfer.</strong>
            Close either side and it breaks. WebRTC has no way to resume across
            a closed connection.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Two peers per room.</strong> No broadcast to multiple
            receivers in the same session.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </article>

      <p class="footnote">
        <a href="/about">About</a> · <a href="/how-it-works">How it works</a> · <a href="/terms">Terms</a> · build <a href="/version.json"><code>{buildShort}</code></a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </body>
</html>
